WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.815
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.115 --> 00:00:09.664
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.964 --> 00:00:17.202
Unit twenty-two: Book Reviews. Today you will judge with gradable and non-gradable adjectives and the right intensifiers.

4
00:00:17.502 --> 00:00:20.026
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.967 --> 00:00:24.118
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:25.984
Review.

7
00:00:26.983 --> 00:00:30.829
Review means a written opinion about a book, film or other work.

8
00:00:31.417 --> 00:00:33.730
The review gave the novel four stars.

9
00:00:34.317 --> 00:00:36.412
Your turn. Say: Review.

10
00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:40.082
Review.

11
00:00:40.817 --> 00:00:41.736
Summary.

12
00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.446
Summary means a short account of the main points.

13
00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.305
A summary should not give away the ending.

14
00:00:48.917 --> 00:00:51.209
Your turn. Say: Summary.

15
00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:55.091
Summary.

16
00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:57.199
Evaluation.

17
00:00:58.183 --> 00:01:02.203
Evaluation means a judgement about how good or effective something is.

18
00:01:02.817 --> 00:01:05.025
Her evaluation was fair and detailed.

19
00:01:05.617 --> 00:01:08.373
Your turn. Say: Evaluation.

20
00:01:11.233 --> 00:01:12.490
Evaluation.

21
00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:14.054
Plot.

22
00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:18.025
Plot means the series of events that make up a story.

23
00:01:18.617 --> 00:01:20.872
The plot kept me guessing until the end.

24
00:01:21.483 --> 00:01:23.764
Your turn. Say: Plot.

25
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:27.357
Plot.

26
00:01:28.117 --> 00:01:29.095
Style.

27
00:01:30.083 --> 00:01:33.453
Style means the particular way in which a writer uses language.

28
00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.038
The style is simple but powerful.

29
00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:39.117
Your turn. Say: Style.

30
00:01:41.967 --> 00:01:42.852
Style.

31
00:01:43.617 --> 00:01:44.792
Recommendation.

32
00:01:45.783 --> 00:01:49.420
Recommendation means advice that something is worth reading or doing.

33
00:01:50.017 --> 00:01:52.306
My recommendation is to read it slowly.

34
00:01:52.917 --> 00:01:55.766
Your turn. Say: Recommendation.

35
00:01:58.600 --> 00:01:59.903
Recommendation.

36
00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:01.407
Critic.

37
00:02:02.417 --> 00:02:05.508
Critic means a person who judges books or art for a living.

38
00:02:06.117 --> 00:02:08.337
The critic praised the opening chapter.

39
00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:11.440
Your turn. Say: Critic.

40
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:15.011
Critic.

41
00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:16.542
Reader.

42
00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.339
Reader means a person who reads a book or text.

43
00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:23.124
Every reader will notice something different.

44
00:02:23.717 --> 00:02:26.183
Your turn. Say: Reader.

45
00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:29.802
Reader.

46
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.088
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:41.410
Visal, a critic writes 'The ending is absolutely brilliant' but 'The pace is very slow.' Why not 'very brilliant'?

48
00:02:42.517 --> 00:02:56.616
Gradable adjectives, such as slow, good or tired, can be more or less: very slow, rather slow. Non-gradable ones, such as brilliant, perfect or impossible, are already extreme.

49
00:02:57.717 --> 00:02:59.693
What intensifiers do we use?

50
00:03:00.783 --> 00:03:16.601
With non-gradable adjectives, use absolutely, utterly, completely or totally: absolutely brilliant. With gradable ones, use very, rather, fairly, extremely: extremely slow.

51
00:03:17.717 --> 00:03:19.089
Any exception?

52
00:03:20.183 --> 00:03:33.148
Really works with both. Quite is tricky: quite good means fairly good, but quite perfect means completely perfect. Do not say very perfect or absolutely good in a review.

53
00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:35.858
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:36.717 --> 00:03:43.093
Grammar focus: Intensifiers: very plus gradable and absolutely plus non-gradable.

55
00:03:43.393 --> 00:03:44.615
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:45.317 --> 00:03:47.363
The ending is absolutely brilliant.

57
00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:49.878
The pace is very slow.

58
00:03:50.783 --> 00:03:53.329
The final chapter is utterly unforgettable.

59
00:03:54.267 --> 00:04:03.711
Use very, rather, extremely with gradable adjectives (slow, good). Use absolutely, utterly, completely with extreme adjectives (brilliant, perfect, impossible).

60
00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:08.112
Be careful! Don't say: The ending is very brilliant.

61
00:04:10.067 --> 00:04:12.647
Say: The ending is absolutely brilliant.

62
00:04:12.947 --> 00:04:15.631
Use absolutely with an extreme adjective.

63
00:04:16.433 --> 00:04:19.213
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.140
Judging a Book.

65
00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:25.309
A good review begins with a short summary of the plot without revealing the ending.

66
00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:31.214
The critic's evaluation then explains why the style is extremely effective or rather dull.

67
00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:36.638
A book that is absolutely brilliant for one reader may be very difficult for another.

68
00:04:37.633 --> 00:04:41.421
A clear recommendation tells the reader whether the book is worth the time.

69
00:04:42.433 --> 00:04:46.662
The best reviews are fair, specific and honest about the critic's own taste.

70
00:04:47.767 --> 00:04:51.734
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:52.583 --> 00:04:54.235
The plot is blank amazing.

72
00:04:54.535 --> 00:04:58.362
A, absolutely. B, very. C, a bit.

73
00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:04.097
The answer is A: absolutely.

74
00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:07.609
A short account of the main points is a blank.

75
00:05:07.909 --> 00:05:11.424
A, summary. B, style. C, plot.

76
00:05:14.967 --> 00:05:16.908
The answer is A: summary.

77
00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:19.748
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:20.048 --> 00:05:29.471
A, The novel is very impossible to put down.. B, The novel is absolutely impossible to put down.. C, The novel is absolutely good to put down..

79
00:05:33.033 --> 00:05:37.123
The answer is B: The novel is absolutely impossible to put down..

80
00:05:38.283 --> 00:05:40.875
Advice that a book is worth reading is a blank.

81
00:05:41.175 --> 00:05:44.805
A, plot. B, recommendation. C, critic.

82
00:05:48.367 --> 00:05:50.796
The answer is B: recommendation.

83
00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:54.774
According to the reading, what should good reviews be?

84
00:05:55.074 --> 00:06:00.237
A, fair, specific and honest. B, long and angry. C, very short.

85
00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:06.937
The answer is A: fair, specific and honest.

86
00:06:08.133 --> 00:06:11.250
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:12.117 --> 00:06:14.290
What should a good book review include?

88
00:06:23.083 --> 00:06:24.549
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:24.849 --> 00:06:45.731
A good book review should include a brief summary of the plot, an honest evaluation of the style and characters, and a clear recommendation. The critic should give examples from the text, avoid spoiling the ending and say who would enjoy the book. A fair review is specific and helps the reader decide.

90
00:06:46.031 --> 00:06:48.031
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:48.917 --> 00:06:56.132
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and intensifiers: very plus gradable and absolutely plus non-gradable.

92
00:06:56.432 --> 00:07:02.021
Today's value is critical awareness. Use technology wisely, and always think for yourself.

93
00:07:02.321 --> 00:07:05.496
See you in Unit twenty-three. Goodbye!

94
00:07:05.796 --> 00:07:08.170
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

